1
00:00:02,169 --> 00:00:05,464
Hey, I'm all packed
and ready to go.

2
00:00:05,506 --> 00:00:06,841
That's right.

3
00:00:06,882 --> 00:00:09,468
Daddy and Uncle Joey
are going on a trip today.

4
00:00:09,510 --> 00:00:11,846
We're going to a conference
in Barbados.

5
00:00:11,887 --> 00:00:12,763
Right?
Uh-huh.

6
00:00:12,805 --> 00:00:14,890
Can you say Barbados?

7
00:00:14,932 --> 00:00:15,933
Barbados.

8
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:22,063
I got to say,
it means so much to me

9
00:00:22,105 --> 00:00:23,899
that you guys are coming
all the way over there

10
00:00:23,941 --> 00:00:25,609
to hear me give my speech.

11
00:00:25,651 --> 00:00:27,068
Ooh, and I have
a surprise.

12
00:00:27,110 --> 00:00:29,947
I had to pull some strings,
but I was able to get everyone

13
00:00:29,989 --> 00:00:32,449
passes to the entire conference.

14
00:00:32,491 --> 00:00:33,492
That's right!

15
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:36,787
These babies will 
get you into all

16
00:00:36,829 --> 00:00:39,331
the paleontology
lectures and seminars.

17
00:00:40,791 --> 00:00:45,128
Do you have anything
that will get us out of them?

18
00:00:45,170 --> 00:00:46,588
CHANDLER:
Yeah, Ross, I mean,

19
00:00:46,630 --> 00:00:47,882
we're excited
to hear the speech,

20
00:00:47,923 --> 00:00:49,466
but the rest of the time,
we're going to want

21
00:00:49,508 --> 00:00:50,968
to do, you know,
island stuff.

22
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:53,052
I think David will probably
want to hear a few lectures.

23
00:00:53,094 --> 00:00:54,304
Oh, right, because
he's a scientist.

24
00:00:54,346 --> 00:00:55,890
No, no, well,
because, you know,

25
00:00:55,931 --> 00:00:57,474
he's been in Minsk
for eight years,

26
00:00:57,516 --> 00:01:00,019
and if he gets too much
direct sunlight, he'll die.

27
00:01:01,562 --> 00:01:03,022
Okay, we better go.

28
00:01:03,104 --> 00:01:05,482
So we'll see
you guys tomorrow.

29
00:01:05,524 --> 00:01:06,525
All right, let's do it.

30
00:01:06,567 --> 00:01:08,027
Five-hour flight with Charlie,

31
00:01:08,068 --> 00:01:10,153
have a couple drinks,
get under that blanket

32
00:01:10,195 --> 00:01:11,989
and do what comes natural.

33
00:01:12,031 --> 00:01:15,534
It's a blanket, Joe,
not a cloak of invisibility.

34
00:01:21,832 --> 00:01:26,921
♪ So no one told you
life was gonna be this way ♪

35
00:01:26,962 --> 00:01:29,172
♪ Your job's a joke,
you're broke ♪

36
00:01:29,214 --> 00:01:31,842
♪ Your love life's D.O.A. ♪

37
00:01:31,884 --> 00:01:35,930
♪ It's like you're always
stuck in second gear ♪

38
00:01:35,971 --> 00:01:38,432
♪ When it hasn't been
your day, your week ♪

39
00:01:38,473 --> 00:01:41,226
♪ Your month,
or even your year ♪

40
00:01:41,268 --> 00:01:45,188
♪ But I'll be there for you ♪

41
00:01:45,230 --> 00:01:48,067
♪ When the rain starts to pour ♪

42
00:01:48,107 --> 00:01:50,444
♪ I'll be there for you ♪

43
00:01:50,485 --> 00:01:52,529
♪ Like I've been there before ♪

44
00:01:52,571 --> 00:01:55,365
♪ I'll be there for you ♪

45
00:01:55,407 --> 00:01:59,620
♪ 'Cause you're there
for me, too. ♪

46
00:02:11,339 --> 00:02:14,551
Wow. This place
is beautiful.

47
00:02:14,593 --> 00:02:17,304
Look at all these
paleontologists!

48
00:02:17,345 --> 00:02:20,223
I know. There are going
to be some pasty folks

49
00:02:20,265 --> 00:02:22,392
by the pool tomorrow.

50
00:02:22,434 --> 00:02:25,479
Oh, my God, I can't
believe you're here.

51
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:26,939
I think I've been recognized.

52
00:02:26,981 --> 00:02:28,148
This happens all the time.

53
00:02:28,189 --> 00:02:31,443
Dr. Geller, I am
such a huge fan.

54
00:02:31,485 --> 00:02:33,779
That never happens.

55
00:02:33,821 --> 00:02:36,281
I've been following
your career for years.

56
00:02:36,323 --> 00:02:38,575
I can't wait for
your keynote speech.

57
00:02:38,617 --> 00:02:41,745
Wow. This is
very flattering.

58
00:02:41,787 --> 00:02:44,164
I would love
your autograph.

59
00:02:44,205 --> 00:02:46,625
Uh... sure.

60
00:02:46,667 --> 00:02:47,835
Um... Dear...

61
00:02:47,876 --> 00:02:48,919
Sarah.

62
00:02:48,961 --> 00:02:50,129
Sarah...

63
00:02:50,169 --> 00:02:54,257
I... dig you.

64
00:02:54,299 --> 00:02:57,594
Dr. Ross Geller.

65
00:02:57,636 --> 00:02:58,720
Oh, thank you so much.

66
00:02:58,762 --> 00:02:59,763
Sure. Oh, and, Sarah,

67
00:02:59,805 --> 00:03:00,889
like to introduce you

68
00:03:00,931 --> 00:03:03,308
to my colleague,
Professor Wheeler.

69
00:03:03,350 --> 00:03:05,102
And this is
Joey Tribbiani.

70
00:03:05,144 --> 00:03:07,062
Are you a paleontologist?

71
00:03:07,104 --> 00:03:08,355
Oh, God, no, no.

72
00:03:08,396 --> 00:03:10,189
No, no, I'm an actor.

73
00:03:10,231 --> 00:03:11,692
You probably recognize me

74
00:03:11,733 --> 00:03:14,069
from a little show called
The Days of Our Lives.

75
00:03:14,111 --> 00:03:17,280
Dude, it's just
Days of Our Lives.

76
00:03:17,322 --> 00:03:19,783
There's no "the."

77
00:03:20,784 --> 00:03:24,705
( chuckling ):
Okay, Ross.
Boy, this guy... yeah.

78
00:03:24,746 --> 00:03:27,582
No, I play Dr. Drake Ramoray.

79
00:03:27,624 --> 00:03:30,085
I'm sorry, I don't own a TV.

80
00:03:30,127 --> 00:03:32,253
You don't own a TV?

81
00:03:32,295 --> 00:03:36,257
What's all your furniture
pointed at?

82
00:03:39,636 --> 00:03:40,888
David, can
you help me?

83
00:03:40,929 --> 00:03:42,597
I'm trying to explain
to Chandler

84
00:03:42,639 --> 00:03:44,265
how a plane stays
in the air.

85
00:03:44,307 --> 00:03:45,684
Oh, certainly.

86
00:03:45,726 --> 00:03:47,435
that's a combination
of Bernoulli's Principle

87
00:03:47,477 --> 00:03:49,813
and Newton's Third Law
of Motion.

88
00:03:49,855 --> 00:03:51,439
See?

89
00:03:51,481 --> 00:03:52,774
Yeah, that's the same as

90
00:03:52,816 --> 00:03:54,317
"it has something
to do with wind."

91
00:03:54,359 --> 00:03:55,610
All right, I'm going to go

92
00:03:55,652 --> 00:03:57,612
pick up a few things
for the trip.

93
00:03:57,654 --> 00:03:59,156
Oh, I should
go, too.

94
00:03:59,198 --> 00:04:01,075
Tomorrow-- do you guys want
to share a cab to the airport,

95
00:04:01,116 --> 00:04:03,118
or should Mike and I
just meet you there?

96
00:04:06,580 --> 00:04:10,375
( laughing ):
Mike? Who's Mike?

97
00:04:11,292 --> 00:04:14,171
Mike's your-your
ex, uh, boyfriend.

98
00:04:14,213 --> 00:04:15,547
That's right! Oh, yeah.

99
00:04:15,589 --> 00:04:18,299
Wow. I had totally
forgotten about him.

100
00:04:18,341 --> 00:04:21,845
That's a... that's a
blast from the past.

101
00:04:21,887 --> 00:04:23,638
It's okay.

102
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:24,723
Honest mistake.

103
00:04:24,765 --> 00:04:26,850
It really doesn't
mean anything.

104
00:04:26,892 --> 00:04:28,227
I mean, you know...

105
00:04:28,268 --> 00:04:30,938
Monica refers to Chandler
as Richard all the time.

106
00:04:32,814 --> 00:04:34,441
She does?

107
00:04:34,482 --> 00:04:36,902
Let's get you out of here.

108
00:04:41,448 --> 00:04:44,201
Well, at least you
took me down with you.

109
00:04:44,243 --> 00:04:45,577
I'm so sorry.

110
00:04:45,619 --> 00:04:47,788
I just... I keep thinking
about Mike.

111
00:04:47,829 --> 00:04:49,289
I'm crazy about David,

112
00:04:49,330 --> 00:04:52,584
and we're having
so much fun together.

113
00:04:52,626 --> 00:04:54,169
Why-why do I miss Mike?

114
00:04:54,211 --> 00:04:56,004
That's just... that's-that's
going to go away, right?

115
00:04:56,046 --> 00:04:58,882
I guess, in time.
Yeah.

116
00:04:58,924 --> 00:05:01,384
I mean, my feelings for
Richard are certainly gone.

117
00:05:01,426 --> 00:05:02,970
You just did it again.

118
00:05:03,011 --> 00:05:07,641
Chandler-- your feelings
for Chandler are certainly gone.

119
00:05:09,059 --> 00:05:10,518
Boy, Phoebe's still
pretty hung up

120
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:11,895
on that Mike, huh?

121
00:05:11,937 --> 00:05:14,522
I wouldn't read
too much into it.

122
00:05:14,564 --> 00:05:15,941
Still, you know,

123
00:05:15,983 --> 00:05:18,277
a girl calls you
by her ex-boyfriend's name,

124
00:05:18,318 --> 00:05:20,154
that's not a good thing,
right?

125
00:05:20,195 --> 00:05:21,571
David, let me stop you there,

126
00:05:21,613 --> 00:05:23,490
'cause I think I see
where this is going.

127
00:05:23,531 --> 00:05:25,284
I'm not very good
at giving advice.

128
00:05:25,325 --> 00:05:28,620
See, if you want advice,
go to Ross, Monica

129
00:05:28,662 --> 00:05:31,665
or Joey if the thing
you want advice about

130
00:05:31,706 --> 00:05:33,000
is pizza toppings

131
00:05:33,041 --> 00:05:34,751
or a burning sensation
when you pee.

132
00:05:34,793 --> 00:05:40,174
Sorry, I just... wish there was
something I could do, you know?

133
00:05:40,215 --> 00:05:41,716
Well-well, you know Phoebe...

134
00:05:41,758 --> 00:05:44,469
Seriously,
we're going to do this?

135
00:05:44,511 --> 00:05:45,470
I'm sorry.

136
00:05:45,512 --> 00:05:46,680
I just wish I could
make her forget

137
00:05:46,721 --> 00:05:47,806
about Mike already,
you know?

138
00:05:47,848 --> 00:05:50,100
Why did Phoebe and Mike
break up?

139
00:05:50,142 --> 00:05:53,687
Because his penis was too big.

140
00:05:55,314 --> 00:05:56,731
( chuckles )

141
00:05:56,773 --> 00:06:01,153
I'm sorry. That's
the kind of thing I do.

142
00:06:01,195 --> 00:06:04,198
They broke up because
Mike didn't want
to get married.

143
00:06:04,239 --> 00:06:06,407
Hey, what if you just
let Phoebe know

144
00:06:06,449 --> 00:06:07,408
you'd be open to marriage?

145
00:06:07,450 --> 00:06:08,869
That's great.

146
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:10,411
That's... that's great.

147
00:06:10,453 --> 00:06:11,913
I'll propose to her.

148
00:06:11,955 --> 00:06:13,540
What?

149
00:06:13,581 --> 00:06:15,917
Well, I was probably
going to do it at some point.

150
00:06:15,959 --> 00:06:16,960
I didn't mean now.

151
00:06:17,002 --> 00:06:18,920
Well, why not?
It's brilliant.

152
00:06:18,962 --> 00:06:20,088
Good-bye, Mike.

153
00:06:20,130 --> 00:06:21,840
We'll see you
at the wedding, fella.

154
00:06:21,882 --> 00:06:24,885
Well, we probably
won't invite you
to the wedding, but...

155
00:06:24,926 --> 00:06:27,262
Thank you, Chandler.
Sincerely, thank you.

156
00:06:27,304 --> 00:06:28,680
Well, you're welcome.

157
00:06:28,722 --> 00:06:30,224
Glad I could help.

158
00:06:31,558 --> 00:06:33,268
How do you think
I should propose?

159
00:06:33,310 --> 00:06:35,187
David, I'm pretending
to read here.

160
00:06:35,229 --> 00:06:37,564
I'm sorry.

161
00:06:40,234 --> 00:06:43,653
Yeah. How you doing? Uh-huh.

162
00:06:43,695 --> 00:06:45,072
Hey, hey.

163
00:06:45,113 --> 00:06:47,699
You said you were
going to wear a thong.

164
00:06:47,741 --> 00:06:48,658
Where's the thong?

165
00:06:48,700 --> 00:06:51,452
I didn't mean a thong.

166
00:06:51,494 --> 00:06:53,663
I meant thongs.

167
00:06:53,705 --> 00:06:57,959
You really should have been
more clear about that.

168
00:06:58,001 --> 00:07:00,503
( knocking )

169
00:07:02,214 --> 00:07:03,631
Hey.
Hey.

170
00:07:03,673 --> 00:07:05,842
You're never going to guess
who I just saw downstairs.

171
00:07:05,884 --> 00:07:08,011
Oh, uh, Britney Spears.

172
00:07:08,053 --> 00:07:11,181
Yeah, she never misses
these conferences.

173
00:07:11,223 --> 00:07:14,101
No, I just saw
Dr. Kenneth Schwartz!

174
00:07:14,142 --> 00:07:15,018
Oh, my God!

175
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:15,769
I know.

176
00:07:15,810 --> 00:07:17,020
Did you talk to him?

177
00:07:17,062 --> 00:07:20,565
Yeah. What am I going to say
to Kenneth Schwartz?

178
00:07:20,607 --> 00:07:21,566
You could say, 
"Hey, Kenny,

179
00:07:21,608 --> 00:07:23,610
how come you're
not Britney Spears?"

180
00:07:24,819 --> 00:07:25,862
You ready to go?

181
00:07:25,904 --> 00:07:27,364
You-you going
to go now?

182
00:07:27,406 --> 00:07:29,283
I thought we
could hang out.

183
00:07:29,324 --> 00:07:31,159
Oh, I can't.
I have seminars all day,

184
00:07:31,201 --> 00:07:33,703
and I promised Ross
I'd look at his speech.

185
00:07:33,745 --> 00:07:36,415
But maybe we could
have dinner later.

186
00:07:36,455 --> 00:07:37,540
Out on the balcony?

187
00:07:37,582 --> 00:07:38,875
It would be romantic.

188
00:07:38,917 --> 00:07:42,670
Will you wear a thong?

189
00:07:42,712 --> 00:07:44,172
I will if you will.

190
00:07:44,214 --> 00:07:49,219
You got yourself
a very weird deal.

191
00:07:51,054 --> 00:07:53,472
I'm good.
I have dinner plans, so...

192
00:07:53,514 --> 00:07:56,101
So, you going
to be okay?

193
00:07:56,143 --> 00:07:57,352
Yeah, yeah.

194
00:07:57,394 --> 00:07:58,645
I got tons of stuff
I could do.

195
00:07:58,686 --> 00:08:01,189
I'm going to hit the beach,
go swimming...

196
00:08:01,231 --> 00:08:03,566
Joe, have you looked outside?

197
00:08:03,608 --> 00:08:06,486
No. Why?

198
00:08:08,613 --> 00:08:09,489
Oh, man!

199
00:08:09,530 --> 00:08:10,698
There's an indoor pool.

200
00:08:10,740 --> 00:08:12,700
You could
swim there.
Yeah.

201
00:08:12,742 --> 00:08:13,701
I wasn't going to swim.

202
00:08:13,743 --> 00:08:15,745
I was going to dig a hole.

203
00:08:20,250 --> 00:08:22,501
Wow, that "Mike" thing
was interesting.

204
00:08:22,543 --> 00:08:25,255
I don't know what's
going to happen with
Phoebe and David.

205
00:08:25,297 --> 00:08:26,256
I do.

206
00:08:26,298 --> 00:08:28,300
Want a hint, huh?

207
00:08:28,883 --> 00:08:30,385
I do.

208
00:08:32,179 --> 00:08:33,680
I do.

209
00:08:34,639 --> 00:08:37,600
Okay, I'm sensing this is
some kind of wordplay,

210
00:08:37,642 --> 00:08:40,687
because you are pink
with barely controlled glee.

211
00:08:40,728 --> 00:08:43,148
David's going
to propose to Phoebe.

212
00:08:43,190 --> 00:08:44,774
What?!

213
00:08:44,816 --> 00:08:46,026
Why?!

214
00:08:46,067 --> 00:08:48,695
Because we were talking
about ways

215
00:08:48,736 --> 00:08:49,988
that he could beat Mike,

216
00:08:50,030 --> 00:08:51,781
and I told him that
Phoebe wanted to get married.

217
00:08:51,823 --> 00:08:54,034
Chandler, we have
talked about this.

218
00:08:54,075 --> 00:08:57,078
You are not supposed
to give people advice.

219
00:08:58,913 --> 00:09:00,081
Now, couldn't
you have made

220
00:09:00,123 --> 00:09:01,540
some sort of
inappropriate joke?

221
00:09:01,582 --> 00:09:02,334
I did!

222
00:09:02,376 --> 00:09:04,336
A penis one.

223
00:09:04,378 --> 00:09:07,297
Just so I know, what was
so wrong about what I said?

224
00:09:07,339 --> 00:09:09,424
They've only been going
out for a few weeks.

225
00:09:09,466 --> 00:09:11,676
Phoebe is completely
hung up on Mike.

226
00:09:11,718 --> 00:09:15,596
She'll say no,
David's heart will be broken,

227
00:09:15,638 --> 00:09:17,598
it'll be too hard for them
to recover from,

228
00:09:17,640 --> 00:09:21,061
and then Phoebe will end up
alone again.

229
00:09:21,102 --> 00:09:24,689
Man, that's some bad advice.

230
00:09:31,029 --> 00:09:31,988
Oh, hey.

231
00:09:32,030 --> 00:09:33,573
Thank God you guys are here.

232
00:09:33,614 --> 00:09:35,534
What-what's going on?

233
00:09:35,574 --> 00:09:37,952
Everything is
upside down here.

234
00:09:37,994 --> 00:09:41,456
It rains all day long,
nobody watches TV,

235
00:09:41,498 --> 00:09:44,209
and Ross is famous.

236
00:09:45,168 --> 00:09:46,836
All right, I don't want
to alarm anybody,

237
00:09:46,878 --> 00:09:49,423
but Monica's hair is
twice as big as it was
when we landed.

238
00:09:49,464 --> 00:09:54,219
Okay, when I go places
with high humidity,

239
00:09:54,261 --> 00:09:57,514
it gets a little
extra body, okay?

240
00:09:57,556 --> 00:10:02,185
That's why our
honeymoon photos look
like me and Diana Ross.

241
00:10:02,227 --> 00:10:04,229
Come on, I'll show you guys
where to check in.

242
00:10:04,271 --> 00:10:07,107
Oh, honey...
make sure we get
a king-size bed.

243
00:10:07,148 --> 00:10:09,317
Oh, David, get one
for us, too.

244
00:10:09,359 --> 00:10:11,945
Oh, and see if they have
a heart-shaped one.

245
00:10:11,986 --> 00:10:14,239
And with mirrors
on the ceiling.

246
00:10:14,281 --> 00:10:19,202
And make sure our room
isn't next to theirs.

247
00:10:19,244 --> 00:10:22,163
Oh, you guys are so lucky
you're here with people.

248
00:10:22,205 --> 00:10:24,583
You know, it's just
such a romantic place.

249
00:10:24,624 --> 00:10:26,709
That's all-- I just
wish I could...

250
00:10:26,751 --> 00:10:29,837
share it with
a guy.

251
00:10:30,671 --> 00:10:32,507
Not Joey.

252
00:10:32,549 --> 00:10:33,925
Not Joey, no.

253
00:10:33,967 --> 00:10:36,677
I was... I was just
lusting after Chandler.

254
00:10:36,719 --> 00:10:38,721
Yeah, right.

255
00:10:41,057 --> 00:10:42,225
So, um...

256
00:10:42,267 --> 00:10:45,770
I'm proposing
to Phoebe tonight.

257
00:10:45,812 --> 00:10:47,855
Tonight?

258
00:10:47,897 --> 00:10:51,067
Isn't an engagement ring
supposed to have a diamond?

259
00:10:52,277 --> 00:10:53,361
Oh, there it is.

260
00:10:53,403 --> 00:10:56,906
Yes, well, being
a failed scientist

261
00:10:56,948 --> 00:10:58,908
doesn't pay quite as well
as you might think.

262
00:10:58,950 --> 00:11:02,120
That's um, that's
1/70 of a karat.

263
00:11:02,162 --> 00:11:05,039
And the clarity is...

264
00:11:05,081 --> 00:11:07,083
is quite poor.

265
00:11:07,125 --> 00:11:09,544
Nice.

266
00:11:10,337 --> 00:11:13,173
Monica, can I talk to you
for a second?

267
00:11:13,214 --> 00:11:14,549
Okay.

268
00:11:14,591 --> 00:11:16,384
David's going to propose
to Phoebe tonight.

269
00:11:16,426 --> 00:11:19,678
See what happens when you
give people advice?

270
00:11:20,388 --> 00:11:22,098
I hope you told him not to.

271
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:23,642
That would be advice!

272
00:11:23,682 --> 00:11:27,521
Okay, fine. I'll handle this.

273
00:11:29,314 --> 00:11:30,148
Phoebe.

274
00:11:30,190 --> 00:11:31,608
Yes?

275
00:11:31,650 --> 00:11:33,360
I need to talk to you.

276
00:11:33,401 --> 00:11:36,821
Are you leaving
the Supremes?

277
00:11:41,784 --> 00:11:43,119
Okay, my husband

278
00:11:43,161 --> 00:11:47,666
just gave your boyfriend
some very bad advice.

279
00:11:47,706 --> 00:11:50,377
David's going to propose
to you tonight.

280
00:11:50,418 --> 00:11:52,462
Wow. Really?

281
00:11:52,504 --> 00:11:56,007
That's fantastic.

282
00:11:56,049 --> 00:11:57,842
What? Are you serious?

283
00:11:57,884 --> 00:11:59,844
You want to marry him?

284
00:11:59,886 --> 00:12:01,012
What about Mike?

285
00:12:01,054 --> 00:12:02,514
Oh, okay, you want me
to marry Mike?

286
00:12:02,556 --> 00:12:05,766
All right, well, let's just
gag him and handcuff him

287
00:12:05,808 --> 00:12:07,810
and force him down
the aisle, okay?

288
00:12:07,852 --> 00:12:09,187
I mean, I can just see it.

289
00:12:09,229 --> 00:12:10,689
"Mike, do you take Phoebe?"

290
00:12:10,771 --> 00:12:13,358
( muffled protest )

291
00:12:13,608 --> 00:12:16,319
You know, it's
every girl's dream.

292
00:12:16,361 --> 00:12:19,239
Do you really think
that marrying someone else

293
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:20,365
is the right answer?

294
00:12:20,407 --> 00:12:21,616
Sure.

295
00:12:21,658 --> 00:12:25,245
Look, okay, bottom line--
I love Mike... David!

296
00:12:25,286 --> 00:12:27,205
Uh... David.

297
00:12:27,247 --> 00:12:29,040
I love David.

298
00:12:29,082 --> 00:12:32,586
Don't look at me that way,
Roseanne Roseannadanna.

299
00:12:35,672 --> 00:12:38,425
"By using CT scans
and computer imaging,

300
00:12:38,466 --> 00:12:42,637
"we can, in a very real way,
bring the Mesozoic Era

301
00:12:42,679 --> 00:12:44,681
into the 21st century."

302
00:12:47,684 --> 00:12:48,809
It's great.

303
00:12:48,851 --> 00:12:50,228
You're going to be the
hit of the conference.

304
00:12:50,270 --> 00:12:52,647
Oh, and you know what?
It'll be even better tomorrow

305
00:12:52,689 --> 00:12:55,275
because I won't be constantly
interrupted by Joey

306
00:12:55,316 --> 00:12:58,737
checking to see if they put
chocolates on my pillow yet.

307
00:12:58,777 --> 00:13:01,281
( knocking )

308
00:13:02,574 --> 00:13:03,575
Hey, guys.

309
00:13:03,617 --> 00:13:05,410
The chocolates
aren't here yet.

310
00:13:05,452 --> 00:13:06,953
Damn it!

311
00:13:06,994 --> 00:13:08,747
Ross just read me
his speech.

312
00:13:08,787 --> 00:13:09,623
It's fantastic.

313
00:13:09,664 --> 00:13:11,165
Oh, is it on the computer?

314
00:13:11,207 --> 00:13:13,293
'Cause I'd love
to give it a read.

315
00:13:13,334 --> 00:13:16,755
If you want to check
your e-mail, just ask.

316
00:13:16,795 --> 00:13:19,215
What? May I?

317
00:13:20,925 --> 00:13:22,677
What's with the rain, Geller?

318
00:13:22,719 --> 00:13:25,012
I mean, when I signed up
for dino week,

319
00:13:25,054 --> 00:13:28,891
nobody said anything
about it being monsoon season.

320
00:13:28,933 --> 00:13:31,269
Actually, the wet season
is June to December.

321
00:13:31,311 --> 00:13:33,104
It's not the time, Charlie.

322
00:13:33,146 --> 00:13:34,897
No, no! Dear God, no!

323
00:13:34,939 --> 00:13:38,985
Oh, what, did
someone outbid you
for the teapot?

324
00:13:40,487 --> 00:13:43,573
Secret teapot?

325
00:13:43,864 --> 00:13:45,784
Your computer,
I don't know wha...

326
00:13:45,824 --> 00:13:46,909
Everything's gone.

327
00:13:46,951 --> 00:13:47,952
What do you mean?

328
00:13:47,994 --> 00:13:49,078
It must be a virus.

329
00:13:49,120 --> 00:13:51,372
I think it erased
your hard drive.

330
00:13:51,414 --> 00:13:52,373
What?

331
00:13:52,415 --> 00:13:54,000
Oh, my God.
What did you do?

332
00:13:54,041 --> 00:13:56,919
Someone I don't know
sent me an e-mail,
and I opened it.

333
00:13:56,961 --> 00:13:58,296
Why? Why would you open it?

334
00:13:58,338 --> 00:14:00,256
Well, it didn't say
"This is a virus."

335
00:14:00,298 --> 00:14:02,841
What did it say?

336
00:14:02,883 --> 00:14:05,052
"Nude...

337
00:14:05,637 --> 00:14:08,389
pictures of Anna Kournikova."

338
00:14:08,431 --> 00:14:11,309
I'm so sorry.

339
00:14:11,351 --> 00:14:12,310
What am I going to do?

340
00:14:12,352 --> 00:14:13,436
My speech is gone, Chandler.

341
00:14:13,478 --> 00:14:14,521
It's not gone.

342
00:14:14,562 --> 00:14:16,063
I mean, I'm sure you
printed out a copy.

343
00:14:16,105 --> 00:14:17,190
You have a hard copy, right?

344
00:14:17,231 --> 00:14:18,650
No, I don't!

345
00:14:18,692 --> 00:14:20,819
Well, you must be
pretty mad

346
00:14:20,860 --> 00:14:23,112
at yourself right now.

347
00:14:33,080 --> 00:14:34,749
It's really gone?

348
00:14:34,791 --> 00:14:36,292
Yep.

349
00:14:36,334 --> 00:14:37,960
I'd like to thank you guys
for coming down here

350
00:14:38,002 --> 00:14:41,589
to complain about the rain
and ruin my career.

351
00:14:41,631 --> 00:14:42,923
I just feel awful.

352
00:14:42,965 --> 00:14:44,050
Yeah, you should.

353
00:14:44,091 --> 00:14:46,093
Nude pictures
of Anna Kournikova?

354
00:14:46,135 --> 00:14:49,639
She's never even won
a major tournament.

355
00:14:49,681 --> 00:14:52,851
Well, I tried
Billie Jean King, but...

356
00:14:54,143 --> 00:14:55,603
You know,
you and Monica have

357
00:14:55,645 --> 00:14:57,564
the same "I'm going
to kill you" look.

358
00:14:57,605 --> 00:15:00,941
I can usually
make it go away
by kissing her.

359
00:15:02,026 --> 00:15:03,403
Get out.

360
00:15:04,987 --> 00:15:06,906
You know, this happens
all the time

361
00:15:06,947 --> 00:15:07,948
to my computer at work.

362
00:15:07,990 --> 00:15:08,991
Well, what do you do?

363
00:15:09,033 --> 00:15:10,535
Well, I usually go play Tetris

364
00:15:10,577 --> 00:15:12,579
on somebody else's computer.

365
00:15:13,287 --> 00:15:14,581
I can't believe this.

366
00:15:14,622 --> 00:15:15,874
I can't believe
this is happening.

367
00:15:15,915 --> 00:15:18,959
I have to give
the keynote speech
tomorrow, okay?

368
00:15:19,001 --> 00:15:20,712
I have to stand up
in front of all these people.

369
00:15:20,754 --> 00:15:21,671
What am I going to say?

370
00:15:21,713 --> 00:15:22,714
I could teach you
a speech

371
00:15:22,756 --> 00:15:24,257
that I memorized
for auditions.

372
00:15:24,298 --> 00:15:25,842
I don't think
your monologue from Star Wars

373
00:15:25,884 --> 00:15:27,761
is going to help me
right now, Joe.

374
00:15:27,802 --> 00:15:30,513
Ross, Ross,
we can solve this.

375
00:15:30,555 --> 00:15:31,848
I just heard your speech.

376
00:15:31,890 --> 00:15:32,849
We can recreate it.

377
00:15:32,891 --> 00:15:34,016
We've got all night.

378
00:15:34,058 --> 00:15:36,436
You really think
we can do that?

379
00:15:36,477 --> 00:15:37,687
Oh, wait.

380
00:15:37,729 --> 00:15:39,814
Joey and I are supposed
to have dinner.

381
00:15:39,856 --> 00:15:41,274
Hey, don't worry about that.

382
00:15:41,315 --> 00:15:42,817
I mean, Ross needs you,
you know?

383
00:15:42,859 --> 00:15:45,319
And Rachel and I will stay
and help any way we can.

384
00:15:45,361 --> 00:15:46,945
( groans )

385
00:15:46,987 --> 00:15:47,864
All right.

386
00:15:47,906 --> 00:15:49,282
Okay, let's do it.

387
00:15:49,323 --> 00:15:52,201
Um, I know we start

388
00:15:52,243 --> 00:15:54,120
by discussing the shortcomings
of carbon dating.

389
00:15:54,161 --> 00:15:57,039
And then, then I move on
to what is clearly

390
00:15:57,081 --> 00:16:00,293
the defining moment
of the Mesozoic Era--

391
00:16:00,334 --> 00:16:02,169
the breakup of Pangaea-- hello.

392
00:16:02,211 --> 00:16:07,258
And then there's the overview
of the Triassic.

393
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:09,636
Ooh, ooh, any chance
any of this happened

394
00:16:09,677 --> 00:16:12,305
in a galaxy far,
far away?

395
00:16:13,473 --> 00:16:15,308
( sputtering )

396
00:16:17,143 --> 00:16:20,020
( phone rings )

397
00:16:20,062 --> 00:16:20,979
Hello?

398
00:16:21,021 --> 00:16:22,022
Okay, Mike,

399
00:16:22,064 --> 00:16:23,357
enough is enough.

400
00:16:23,399 --> 00:16:24,567
Now, you love Phoebe
and she loves you,

401
00:16:24,609 --> 00:16:26,861
so you need to get over
your whole

402
00:16:26,903 --> 00:16:29,155
"I never want to get
married" thing 
and step up!

403
00:16:29,196 --> 00:16:31,324
Who is this?

404
00:16:31,365 --> 00:16:34,118
This is Monica.
I'm Phoebe's friend.

405
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:36,203
Listen, Phoebe is back
with David,

406
00:16:36,245 --> 00:16:37,497
and he's going
to propose to her,

407
00:16:37,538 --> 00:16:38,706
and she's going to say yes,

408
00:16:38,748 --> 00:16:40,500
but I know she really wants
to be with you.

409
00:16:41,793 --> 00:16:44,796
He's, he's going to propose?

410
00:16:46,046 --> 00:16:47,465
I'm sorry.
Did you say something?

411
00:16:47,507 --> 00:16:50,217
I can't hear through
all this damned hair!

412
00:16:50,844 --> 00:16:54,388
Look, if Phoebe wants
to marry David, she should.

413
00:16:54,430 --> 00:16:56,056
I'm not going to stand
in the way of that.

414
00:16:56,098 --> 00:16:57,099
Neither should you.

415
00:16:57,141 --> 00:16:58,559
You don't tell me what to do.

416
00:16:58,601 --> 00:17:01,228
I tell you what to do.

417
00:17:01,270 --> 00:17:02,480
Just call her.

418
00:17:02,522 --> 00:17:04,858
She's at the Paradise Hotel
in Barbados.

419
00:17:04,899 --> 00:17:06,943
And while I've got you,
you've got curly hair.

420
00:17:06,985 --> 00:17:08,987
What do you do in the humidity?

421
00:17:11,071 --> 00:17:12,072
Damn it!

422
00:17:14,993 --> 00:17:16,995
Well, I hope you're happy.

423
00:17:17,036 --> 00:17:19,831
Aw, I hope you're
happy too, honey.

424
00:17:19,873 --> 00:17:22,333
Phoebe's going to say yes
to David.

425
00:17:22,375 --> 00:17:25,043
See? That's what happens when
you meddle in people's lives.

426
00:17:25,085 --> 00:17:26,838
Phoebe's going
to say yes?
That's great.

427
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:28,548
No, it's not! Because
she's still in love with Mike!

428
00:17:28,589 --> 00:17:30,174
And there's no chance
that will work?

429
00:17:30,216 --> 00:17:32,051
No, I called him.

430
00:17:32,092 --> 00:17:33,135
It's not going to happen.

431
00:17:33,177 --> 00:17:34,428
( gasps ):
Meddler! Meddler!

432
00:17:34,470 --> 00:17:37,765
Well, if you hadn't meddled
to start with,

433
00:17:37,807 --> 00:17:39,350
then I wouldn't have had
to go in there

434
00:17:39,392 --> 00:17:40,393
and meddle myself!

435
00:17:40,434 --> 00:17:41,602
Now no matter
how much we meddle,

436
00:17:41,644 --> 00:17:44,146
we'll never be able
to unmeddle the thing

437
00:17:44,188 --> 00:17:46,315
that you meddled up
in the first place!

438
00:17:46,357 --> 00:17:49,736
This vacation sucks!

439
00:17:54,866 --> 00:17:56,450
I'm so bored.

440
00:17:56,492 --> 00:17:58,036
Mm.

441
00:17:58,077 --> 00:17:59,370
Stupid rain.

442
00:17:59,412 --> 00:18:01,247
We can't do anything.

443
00:18:01,288 --> 00:18:03,290
Well, I brought some books.

444
00:18:03,332 --> 00:18:04,500
We could read.

445
00:18:04,542 --> 00:18:07,045
Hey, it hasn't come
to that yet, okay?

446
00:18:07,086 --> 00:18:09,630
Hey, hey, hey,
don't mind if I do.

447
00:18:09,672 --> 00:18:10,798
I'm sorry, sir.

448
00:18:10,840 --> 00:18:14,844
These are for the 
pharmaceutical convention.

449
00:18:14,886 --> 00:18:17,847
Hey, Rach, you feel like
going to a convention?

450
00:18:17,889 --> 00:18:20,474
We can't.
We're not pharmacists.

451
00:18:20,516 --> 00:18:22,727
I know we're not,
but, uh...

452
00:18:22,769 --> 00:18:24,687
Frank Madeo and...

453
00:18:24,729 --> 00:18:31,235
Ava Churara...
wumba... wum...

454
00:18:31,277 --> 00:18:32,361
"Kate Miller."

455
00:18:32,403 --> 00:18:34,572
Kate Miller it is.

456
00:18:34,614 --> 00:18:36,783
Okay.

457
00:18:36,824 --> 00:18:38,868
And that's the most sex

458
00:18:38,910 --> 00:18:41,662
I'm going to have
this weekend.

459
00:18:41,704 --> 00:18:42,830
Well, in that case,

460
00:18:42,872 --> 00:18:45,083
should I make sure
it's on real good?

461
00:18:49,921 --> 00:18:53,299
Thank you.
You're welcome.

462
00:18:54,300 --> 00:18:56,761
Uh, and then, and then
you said that thing

463
00:18:56,803 --> 00:18:58,554
about, about bringing
the Mesozoic Era

464
00:18:58,596 --> 00:19:00,598
into the 21st century.

465
00:19:00,640 --> 00:19:02,224
Well, yeah. That's it?

466
00:19:02,266 --> 00:19:03,017
Yeah.

467
00:19:03,059 --> 00:19:05,436
Oh, my God!
We did it!

468
00:19:05,478 --> 00:19:07,021
Actually,
I did it, Ross.

469
00:19:07,063 --> 00:19:10,024
You remembered
shockingly little
of your own speech.

470
00:19:10,066 --> 00:19:11,776
Yes, but I did make
a pyramid

471
00:19:11,818 --> 00:19:14,028
out of the bath products.

472
00:19:14,070 --> 00:19:16,864
This is, this is amazing.

473
00:19:16,906 --> 00:19:20,660
Thank you.
Thank you so much.

474
00:19:23,245 --> 00:19:25,832
That, that's a pretty necklace.

475
00:19:27,750 --> 00:19:28,918
Thank you.

476
00:19:28,960 --> 00:19:32,088
Hey, what do you say
we celebrate?

477
00:19:32,130 --> 00:19:33,131
Champagne?

478
00:19:33,172 --> 00:19:35,633
Oh, yeah!
Hey, save the cork,

479
00:19:35,675 --> 00:19:37,135
and that way, we can fill
the bottle with water

480
00:19:37,176 --> 00:19:39,178
and put it back
so they don't charge you.

481
00:19:41,931 --> 00:19:44,600
Oh, my God, I love you.

482
00:19:44,642 --> 00:19:47,436
Oh, this is such a cute picture
of Emma.

483
00:19:47,478 --> 00:19:49,105
And is this your son

484
00:19:49,147 --> 00:19:51,149
or just some kid whose picture
you bring on vacation?

485
00:19:51,190 --> 00:19:54,360
Yeah, that's Ben,
my son from my first marriage.

486
00:19:54,401 --> 00:19:55,736
Your first marriage?
Yeah.

487
00:19:55,778 --> 00:19:58,156
You've been married
more than once?

488
00:19:58,197 --> 00:20:01,325
No, no.

489
00:20:03,995 --> 00:20:05,705
So, why'd you break up?

490
00:20:05,746 --> 00:20:07,581
Oh, it was...

491
00:20:07,623 --> 00:20:11,251
Oh, it was...

492
00:20:11,293 --> 00:20:12,294
complicated, you know?

493
00:20:12,336 --> 00:20:15,882
She, uh, she was...

494
00:20:15,923 --> 00:20:17,216
gay.

495
00:20:17,257 --> 00:20:18,759
Oh, my God!

496
00:20:18,801 --> 00:20:19,969
This is so cool!

497
00:20:20,011 --> 00:20:23,764
Okay, odd thing
to get excited about.

498
00:20:23,806 --> 00:20:25,850
No, it's just that
I was engaged to a guy

499
00:20:25,892 --> 00:20:27,101
who turned out to be gay.

500
00:20:27,143 --> 00:20:29,687
Hey! High five!

501
00:20:30,730 --> 00:20:32,065
Didn't you feel
so stupid

502
00:20:32,106 --> 00:20:33,482
that you didn't
see the signs?

503
00:20:33,524 --> 00:20:36,110
My fiance was always going
away on these long weekends

504
00:20:36,152 --> 00:20:37,111
with his tennis partner.

505
00:20:37,153 --> 00:20:39,197
My wife had
a workout friend

506
00:20:39,238 --> 00:20:41,115
she went
to the gym with

507
00:20:41,157 --> 00:20:42,575
every day for a year.

508
00:20:42,616 --> 00:20:44,451
She didn't get
any fitter.

509
00:20:44,493 --> 00:20:45,745
Right, and everybody
finds out,

510
00:20:45,786 --> 00:20:47,788
and they're, like,
"Oh, I knew all along."

511
00:20:47,830 --> 00:20:48,915
I know. If you knew,

512
00:20:48,956 --> 00:20:50,583
why didn't you tell me,
you know?

513
00:20:50,624 --> 00:20:51,918
I mean, call, leave a note.

514
00:20:51,959 --> 00:20:54,962
"Hi, just dropped by
to say your wife's gay."

515
00:20:55,004 --> 00:20:56,463
I know!

516
00:20:56,505 --> 00:20:59,050
And then you try to make
the best of a bad situation

517
00:20:59,092 --> 00:21:03,096
so you float the idea
of a threesome.

518
00:21:03,137 --> 00:21:04,638
I didn't do that.

519
00:21:05,973 --> 00:21:07,767
Me, neither.

520
00:21:11,145 --> 00:21:13,814
Well, who knew?
Pharmacists are fun.

521
00:21:13,856 --> 00:21:14,732
I know.

522
00:21:14,774 --> 00:21:15,733
That old lady
at the end

523
00:21:15,775 --> 00:21:17,193
